# =============================================================================
# reducta via prima (AIR lane) — bounded-source admission rows.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • the operator admits on the AIR lane only over statically bounded
#     sources (a statically shaped tensor or matrix, the zero-extent shape
#     included) — admission needs the bound, not an unroll
#   • a statically zero-extent source is a bounded route and admits; the
#     `nihil` contract is carried unchanged through the gate
#   • reverse AD is forward-only v1: the operator never becomes an AIR tensor
#     op; the other reduction operators stay outside the AIR subset
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • expecting an unbounded source to admit — the gate declines a source
#     without a static extent with SEM059 `reducta_first_unbounded_source`
#     (every tensor or matrix a signature can name is bounded, so the decline
#     is a guard rather than a row an exemplar can show)
#   • expecting `summa` or another reduction operator to admit — only `prima`
#     is admitted on the AIR lane; the rest stay outside the v1 subset
#
# See also: reducta, nihil, tensor
# =============================================================================

# Statically shaped tensor parameter admits.
@ radix lane "air"
functio elige(tf32[4] x) → fractus ∪ nihil {
    redde reducta via prima ex x sit v { redde v ≻ 0.0 }
}

# Zero extent — a statically empty source admits; result is nihil.
@ radix lane "air"
functio elige_vacuum(tf32[0] x) → fractus ∪ nihil {
    redde reducta via prima ex x sit v { redde v ≻ 0.0 }
}

# Matrix — a static two-axis shape admits, with its coordinates bound.
@ radix lane "air"
functio elige_cellam(tf32[2, 2] x) → fractus ∪ nihil {
    redde reducta via prima ex x apud [i, j] sit v { redde v ≻ 0.0 }
}
